00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.060
When  working with spreadsheets,
there may be occasions where you need

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.960
to have that information sorted in a
specific format, such as alphabetically,

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.510
by date or numerical value.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.470
The easiest way to do that
within spreadsheets is to

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.910
use the sort and filter tool.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:19.260
And as you can see I have some
information in this sample spreadsheet

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.510
in front of me, and I'm just going to
demonstrate how you can apply some sort

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.150
and filter options to the information.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.330
So the first thing we're going
to do is we're going to sort the

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.090
information alphabetically by country.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.059
So if I just click on A and
it highlights all of column A.

00:00:37.769 --> 00:00:38.940
And I'm on the home tab.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:43.230
So if I then just go up to the sort
and filter tool in the top right-hand

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:47.760
corner and select the little drop
down there and I can sort, it, it

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.840
A to Z, Z to A or custom sort and
I'm simply going to sort it A to Z.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.440
Now, once I've done that, Excel
has given me a warning message.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:00.610
And the reason it's doing that is
that is other information that is

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.200
linked to the data within column A.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.520
So it's asking me, do I want to
include that data, which is yes.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:11.920
Otherwise that won't be a true reflection
of what was sent to the USA or Brazil.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:17.620
So I'll tick the expand the selection
button and select sort, and that

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.220
automatically sorts everything
alphabetically, but all of the

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.259
information has been sorted as well.

00:01:24.195 --> 00:01:26.865
It may be a case that you wish
to sort something by date.

00:01:27.405 --> 00:01:34.895
And again, if I select column D
and select sort oldest to newest, I

00:01:34.895 --> 00:01:37.775
will still get the warning message
because it needs to know that.

00:01:38.105 --> 00:01:42.245
Do I want to include this information
or do I just sort the day column?

00:01:42.515 --> 00:01:46.325
If I did that, this information
would no longer be accurate because

00:01:46.325 --> 00:01:48.075
it would not link up with the dates.

00:01:48.095 --> 00:01:50.495
So yes, I do want to expand the selection.

00:01:51.015 --> 00:01:55.175
Select sort, and it will sort
everything on date value.

00:01:57.215 --> 00:02:01.235
As well as using the sort tool, Excel also
has the filter tool, which allows you to

00:02:01.235 --> 00:02:06.125
filter information so you can look for
specific elements within that information.

00:02:07.025 --> 00:02:09.365
Again, this is on the sort and filter tab.

00:02:09.574 --> 00:02:14.585
So what I need to do is I'm going
to select country and I'm going to

00:02:14.615 --> 00:02:19.470
click on the filter button, and that
will apply some filters to the top.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:26.850
So as you can see, there are now dropdown
icons next to the text on row one.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:31.910
So if I click on the little dropdown,
this gives me the filter option, so

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.050
I can now select all, or if it was a
case that I needed to find out which

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:38.820
items were sold to certain countries.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.050
So I would just untick that.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:46.060
And I'll just for example, select
three of these and select OK.

00:02:46.725 --> 00:02:49.065
And those are the countries
so it hides everything else.

00:02:49.635 --> 00:02:53.055
And I'm only seeing information
relating to what was sold in

00:02:53.055 --> 00:02:54.855
Angola, Bulgaria and Cameroon.

00:02:56.255 --> 00:02:59.565
Excel identifies that there is
a filter applied by giving the

00:02:59.565 --> 00:03:01.275
filter icon in the top corner.

00:03:01.575 --> 00:03:04.935
So just so you're aware if you
needed to clear that you can

00:03:04.935 --> 00:03:08.175
select clear filter from country
and it will all go back to normal.

00:03:08.175 --> 00:03:11.805
So at a  glance, you could tell whether
there was a filter applied or not.

00:03:12.195 --> 00:03:17.144
If the filter icon was highlighted
in any of the column headings.

00:03:18.375 --> 00:03:22.005
So for this one, what I'm going to do, I'm
just going to demonstrate again, I've got

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:28.325
item type here, so I just need to see what
was sold, beverages, cereal and clothes.

00:03:28.695 --> 00:03:33.524
And I can apply other filters so
I may need to know, which was sold

00:03:33.555 --> 00:03:34.875
online, which was sold offline.

00:03:34.875 --> 00:03:38.595
So I'll just select online,
so I've ticked offline.

00:03:39.015 --> 00:03:42.929
So there was four sold  online
and it may be a case that I  need

00:03:42.929 --> 00:03:45.960
to see what was sold in 2012.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.340
So I'll untick 2014/17.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:49.860
Select that.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.780
So I know there were two items
sold in those countries online.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:55.380
And in that year.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:04:00.300
Again, if I just highlighted the
first country and then went to the

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.980
sort and filter, if I untick that
it would remove all of the filters

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:10.740
from the column headings, and then
your data is all back to normal.

